Subject: Keep the focus of an app set to keep game focus while another app's window has it

DSA shows each task in its own host window, and the guest's tasks never cover each other, so a
task that loses the focus to another app's task stays visible and resumed. Android still tells
its app that it lost the focus, in two ways: the activity is no longer the top resumed one
(Activity.onTopResumedActivityChanged(false), from the TopResumedActivityChangeItem the window
manager sends when another task becomes the top resumed one), and its window lost the window
focus (Activity.onWindowFocusChanged(false), which ViewRootImpl hands to the views when the input
dispatcher moves the focus to another window). Games pause on these: a Unity game such as
Monument Valley pauses on onWindowFocusChanged(false), which is also why this file already gives
an unfocused Unity app a fake focus in split screen (dispatchCompatFakeFocus). So the game stops
whenever the user clicks another app's window, although it is still in view.

A user can set an app to keep game focus. The window manager's shell command "dsa-keep-focus
set [PACKAGE...]" sets which packages do; the DSA agent, which holds MANAGE_ACTIVITY_TASKS, sets
the packages the user switched on, when it starts and whenever the user changes them. Without
arguments the command prints "supported", which is how the agent tells an image with this change
from one without it.

Every TopResumedActivityChangeItem now says whether the activity's package keeps its focus
(ActivityRecord.dsaKeepFocus), and the app's process keeps that on the activity's record
(ActivityClientRecord.mDsaKeepFocus). For such an activity, while it stays resumed:

- ActivityThread.handleTopResumedActivityChanged does not report the loss of the top resumed
  state to the activity. It still records the state, and the item still tells the window
  manager the loss was handled (activityTopResumedStateLost), so the next top activity does not
  wait for it. The activity hears the loss when it pauses, before onPause, as Android always
  reports it then (performPauseActivityIfNeeded), and not at all if it is the top resumed one
  again before that.
- ViewRootImpl does not tell the views of its windows (the activity's own and its dialogs') that
  the window lost the window focus while another app's task is the top resumed one and the
  window is visible. The window manager moves the top resumed activity before the window focus,
  so the word that another task is on top normally comes first; when the loss of window focus
  comes first, it waits up to 300 ms for that word, and the views are told after all without it
  (the focus went to another window of the same app). When the window gets the focus back, the
  views are not told either, since they never heard they lost it. When the window is hidden, the
  views are told of the loss then.

Only what the app hears changes. The window focus itself follows the input dispatcher as before:
mAttachInfo.mHasWindowFocus, the IME's served window, the input stages and pointer capture take
the loss at once, so keys and IME text go only to the window that has the focus. Nothing changes
for an app that is not set, nor while no package is set, which is the default.

Applies to frameworks/base at 94b4c163b7dfe5ce3607f7bb8456f9573f7de57d (Android 17), after 0010.
